A||||7||Kage86GT4SP||Not just an "idle" question!
Z||000000||Kage86GT4SP||07-02-99||02:46 PM||||Help me out, folks...<p>My sixer won't idle below around 1800 rpm, regardless of engine temp.<p>This did not happen over a period of time... It ran fine until I hosed out the engine compartment.  Then, I got a check engine light along with a rythmic, 300 rpm idle variation, like someone was pressing the gas pedal every couple of seconds.<p>Now, no CEL, and a constant idle of 1800 rpm or so, after the engine initially starts, idles briefly at around 3200, drops as the engine warms up, but then doesn't get any lower then the 1800 that it holds steady, no matter what I do.<p>likely suspects?  which components?  Do I need a faith healer?  <p>post here, or email, whichever...<p>thanks!<p>Kage (kage@pacifier.com)
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||07-02-99||03:46 PM||||With regards to the "rythmic, 300 rpm idle variation, like someone was pressing the gas pedal every couple of seconds.", a friend of mine has the exact same problem on his 88 Olds Cutlass Supreme, which also has a 2.8L V6. I asked him what he thought the problem was, and he replied:<p>a) computer update needed for GM 2.8L engines to re-route mass airflow sensor..<br>b) dirty airfilter<br>c) failing O2 sensor..<p>any of these likely? Probably not the first one, as I've never heard that mentioned in relation to Fieros before.
Z||000002||Kage86GT4SP||07-02-99||08:05 PM||||Just for the heck of it, I ran the codes (got the following the first time:<p>21, 22-Throttle position sensor<p>34 - MAP sensor voltage low.<p>35 - Idle Air Control + - 50 RPM for 30 seconds (replace)<p>42 - Electronic Spark Timing, HEI module malfunction)<p>I cleared the codes, ran the engine, got the fluctuating RPM again, and a CEL.  I turned the engine off, and then turned it back on again.  This time, no fluctuation and no CEL... just the high idle.<p>I ran the codes again... and the only one that came up was 21.<p>standing by!<p>K (kage@pacifier.com)
Z||000003||theogre||07-02-99||09:13 PM||||He washed the motor, and problems started. Why am I not suprised?<p>Was the motor hot when you hosed it? Likely you cracked something open if it was. Never wash a hot Fiero motor. Those weldup manifolds are especially touchy about crash cooling.<p>If it was cold, the it's likely you got water into something you shouldn't have. Check over all the connectors, especially the block next to the batery as most of the harness goes thru there.
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-08-99||11:59 AM||||Sounds like a cracked EGR tube
Z||000005||vurbanson||07-08-99||04:10 PM||||Hey<p>I have a similar problem with my Fiero, although it's not quite as bad.  The person who suggested the throttle position sensor may be right.  My Fiero tends to idle at at least 1500 rpm for a very long time after I start it.  The RPMs don't drop for up to twenty minutes.  I ran the computer check once and it too said TPS.  I suppose the only other possibility is the oxigen sensor - someone told me that this is very likely.<p>Voytek
Z||000006||theogre||07-08-99||11:23 PM||||On the TPS...... If you haven't changed it yet for some other reason, Do so. There is no such thing as a TPS that is good after a decade+. This is one sensor that does truly wear out.<p>The TPS is basically the same component as the Volume knob on most radios. Ever have a radio or TV crackle as you changed the volume? same thing happens to TPS sensors. ANY water in this sensor Will change it's finktion (spelling intentional). Because of the TPS design, it could hold water that get into it for a long time.<p>This applies to both 4 & 6 cyl motors.
Z||000007||Kage86GT4SP||07-09-99||12:41 AM||||TPS replacement it is!  I'll change it next week and let you all know how it turns out.<p>Thanks for all your help, gang.<p>K.
